extraction_notes: "WB19 Australia printed 119-121 (cache p120-p122, cache page = printed folio + 1), read off the text layer in column order. Heading Technologist O.C.C., by Ben Lucas & Kevin Siembieda, and the lore on printed 119; the stat block on printed 120 prints TWO skill blocks, City Technologist and Outback Technologist, with one Secondary Skills paragraph and one Standard Equipment paragraph marked same for both; equipment ends and Money and Cybernetics are on printed 121, above the Military O.C.C.s heading. || CITY RULES: the City O.C.C. list on printed 105 places the class under Optional City O.C.C.s and prints that every Tech-City character speaks English at a minimum of 84+1D6% and is literate (+20%); the class block prints its own higher figures, which are the ones stored. The same page says a City character played outside the walls is an agent, exile, criminal, rebel or truant, or part of an all-City group; that is in GM Notes. Imported as PLAYABLE on the survey decision of 2026-10-01. || GROUP: a City O.C.C. that is not military; occ_group optional. No Base S.D.C. is printed, so men_of_arms is false (not a Men at Arms class; the Military O.C.C.s heading starts after it on printed 121). || XP: the Raider, Technologist, Guide/Scout/Hunter & Bushman ladder on the Experience Tables, printed 224, read off a render. || ONE CLASS, TWO VARIANTS: the two printed blocks are stored as the variants city and outback. The class body holds the eleven skills both blocks grant at the CITY percentages; the outback variant restates the nine that differ (Speaks English 80% +2D6% is stored as 80, the 2D6 is rolled by the player) and adds its language, Archeology (catalog Archaeology), Anthropology, two lores and Outback Survival; the city variant adds Computer Repair, Photography or Surveillance, two pilot skills and one lore. Starting money and the number of related picks (7 and 6) are per variant. || NOT STORED, a variant cannot carry it: the OUTBACK block''s own O.C.C. Related list (its categories, restrictions and bonuses, printed in the body under Outback Related Skills) and its levels for new skills (3, 6, 9 and 12). The categories, bonuses and schedule stored are the City block''s (two more at 3, 5, 9 and 14), so an Outback Technologist is offered the City list. The City floor of three skills from Communications or Military is NOT stored as a minimum either, because it would bind the Outback variant, which has no such floor; it is in the related note. A separate outback class would be the exact form; that is Nate''s call. || READINGS: Medical: Any; particularly Cybernetics (+10%) is stored as +10 on the whole category; the other reading puts the +10 on the cybernetics skills alone. Hand to Hand upgraded to Expert at the cost of two other skills is costs expert 2; no other style is priced, so none is offered. Read Sensory Instruments is Sensory Equipment. Literacy: English 98% is Literacy: Native Language at a flat 98. Horsemanship General and Exotic Animals are offered at the Pilot +10% because this book files horsemanship under Pilot (the Technologist is a civilian, not one of the City troops printed 157 says are not trained to ride). Cowboy: None and Wilderness: None are honoured by leaving the categories out. || MONEY: City 1D6x$1,000 (the text layer prints lD6x$ 1,000) plus an annual income of 3D6x$3,000, 30-50% more for a Level 4 or 5 citizen. Outback 1D4x$1,000 in tradeable goods and parts and 3D6x$100 in dollars, with an income of 1D6x$500 a month. starting_money holds the coin (1D6x1000, and 3D6x100 on the outback variant); goods and income are in GM Notes. || EQUIPMENT not stored as rows: the home electronics lab, a photo camera (only the digital and video cameras have Rifts rows), wristwatch, clipboard, small camera, radiation detector, lab coat, protective gloves, sample bags and small tools, the field vehicle with its mobile lab, and the City variant''s cybernetics (equipment is shared by both variants) are in GM Notes."
---

## Lore

A Technologist finds, salvages, rebuilds and improves machines. Some are
engineers and scientists, some are scavengers, mechanics and collectors, and
all of them are drawn to gadgets, electronics and software. Since the Great
Cataclysm, recovering lost technology has become a calling of its own.

In Perth and Melbourne the Technologist is an electrical or mechanical
engineer or a research scientist, working to keep the old technology running
and to add to it; the cities hold that technology is what separates them from
the Outback. Outside the walls the Technologist is a tinkerer, historian and
pack-rat who digs through ruins for manuals, parts and components and makes
life a little easier for the people around him. Wilderness communities treat
such a person almost as a priest of machinery. Either kind is inquisitive,
intelligent and creative. Major psychics with Telemechanics often take up the
trade.

## Outback Related Skills

The Outback Technologist selects six other skills from this list, and two
more at levels 3, 6, 9 and 12 (printed 120). The picker shows the City list,
so apply these by hand. Communication: any (+10%). Cowboy: none. Domestic: any
(+10%). Electrical: any (+10%). Espionage: Escape Artist, Forgery,
Intelligence and Pick Locks only (+5%). Mechanical: any (+15%). Medical:
Paramedic only (+5%). Military: any (+5%). Physical: any except Acrobatics,
Gymnastics and Wrestling. Pilot: any (+10%). Pilot Related: any (+10%).
Rogue: Card Sharp only. Science: any (+5%). Technical: any (+10%). W.P.: any.
Wilderness: any.

## GM Notes

The City list on printed 105 says a City character met outside the walls is a
City agent, an exile, a criminal, a rebel or someone with a secret way in and
out, unless the whole group is a City team on assignment.

A City Technologist must take three of the seven starting related skills from
Communications or Military. The sheet does not enforce it.

Kit beyond what the sheet lists (printed 120-121): a larger and better
equipped home electronics lab; a photo camera in place of the digital or video
one if preferred; wristwatch, clipboard, small camera, radiation detector, lab
coat, protective gloves and an array of small sample bags and tools; other
equipment, spare parts and components at the G.M.''s discretion. A City
Technologist of social Level 4 or 5 may be highly placed, with access to a
full electronics and computer lab with assistants and perhaps cybernetics,
robotics, E-Clip rechargers and explosives; one of Level 0-3, and any
Outbacker, works mostly from a small self-funded home lab with perhaps one
assistant. A Technologist who works in the field has a van, truck or similar
vehicle holding a mobile electronics lab with its own energy source.

Cybernetics: the City Technologist starts with a Universal Headjack and Ear
Implant and 1D4+1 further cybernetic enhancements of choice (no bionics),
usually eye implants, image enhancement, macro-vision, tools, scanners and
other sensory augmentation. The Outback Technologist starts with none.

Money: the City Technologist has an annual income of 3D6x$3,000, 30-50% more
at Level 4 or 5. The Outback Technologist also starts with 1D4x$1,000 in
tradeable goods and parts and can earn 1D6x$500 a month; communities that
cannot pay in full tend to repay in favors, information and salvaged parts.

Printed 120 points to Coalition War Campaign for more technical skills.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'technologist');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'technologist';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-technologist-class.sql');
